2. The Regional Government in implementing the handling of special protection of Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 9

1. The Regional Government shall implement integrated services for Victims of acts of Violence as referred to in Article 2 paragraph (2) letter b through activities:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. counseling;
d. psychological services;
e. spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation services;
g. legal aid services and legal assistance;
h. safe house services; and/or
i. repatriation and social reintegration.
2. In the event that the Regional Government does not yet have counseling personnel as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, it may request assistance from social workers, volunteer companions, and/or spiritual counselors.
3. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and Safe Houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure needed for the recovery of victims, including perpetrators.
4. The provision of services to Victims of acts of Violence shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with P2TP2A and other social institutions.
5. P2TP2A and other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (4) may receive and send case referrals from other service units in a networked manner.
6. Integrated Services for Victims of acts of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), paragraph (3), paragraph (4), and paragraph (5) shall be implemented in accordance with standard operating procedures.

Article 26

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 25, include:
a. establishing, implementing policies, programs, and conducting cooperation activities in the implementation of protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutions for the protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence according to regional financial capacity;
d. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence;
e. providing protection services for Women and Child Victims of acts of Violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for Children who are Victims of acts of Violence.

Article 2

1. The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. the rights of women and children who are victims of violence;
b. protection of women who are victims of acts of violence;
c. child protection;
d. P2TP2A;
e. cooperation, obligations, and responsibilities;
f. information and reporting systems;
g. guidance and supervision; and
h. funding.
2. Protection of Women as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b includes:
a. prevention of acts of Violence;
b. integrated services for Victims of acts of Violence; and
c. empowerment of Victims of acts of Violence.
3. Child Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c includes:
a. Child Protection for Children who become Victims of acts of Violence; and
b. Special Protection for children.

Article 11

1. Health services as referred to in Article 9 paragraph (1) letter b, shall be implemented using health facilities owned by the Regional Government or other referral hospitals by providing treatment and health recovery services to Victims.
2. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be performed by health workers to Victims in accordance with professional standards, standard operating procedures and the medical needs of Victims.
3. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1), through efforts: a. taking anamnesis from the Victim;
b. examining the Victim;
c. providing first aid;
d. restoring health both physically and psychologically, undertaking efforts to prevent disease and other health disorders;
e. counseling;
f. referring to more adequate health service facilities if necessary may refer to other parties with a multidisciplinary approach, including health services, medicolegal services, psychosocial assistance and legal aid; and/or g. providing information to the police.
4. In addition to the efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) for certain cases, health workers may provide reproductive health services in accordance with medical needs.
5. In providing services as referred to in paragraph (3) and paragraph (4), health workers must create medical records in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
6. For every medical action to be performed by health workers there must be consent for the medical action from the Victim or their family in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
7. For the purposes of investigation, authorized health workers must prepare a visum et repertum and/or visum et repertum psichiatricum or prepare a medical certificate.
8. Health workers have the right to obtain legal protection.

Article 24

1. In certain cases the Regional Government may cooperate with:
a. Other regional governments;
b. Police;
c. Advocates;
d. health workers;
e. psychologists;
f. social workers;
g. volunteer companions;
h. spiritual counselors;
i. Other law enforcers, to assist Victims in the court hearing process;
j. National Commission on Violence Against Women;
k. Indonesian Child Protection Commission or Child Protection Institution; and/or
l. certain parties desired in the interests of the Victim.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. socialization;
c. provision of legal aid;
d. health services;
e. psychiatric/psychological services;
f. victim assistance;
g. Counseling;
h. social rehabilitation;
i. spiritual guidance; and/or
j. repatriation and social reintegration.

CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 3

Women and Children who are Victims of acts of Violence have rights, including:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth respected as human beings;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable, and in accordance with needs;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain ease/facilitation in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance;
j. the right to a sense of security; and
k. the right to restitution.

Article 4

Child Victims of acts of Violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 3, also receive special rights, including:
a. the right to survival, growth, and development;
b. the right to basic population administration services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 22

1. P2TP2A as referred to in Article 21 has the duties to:
a. provide services;
b. provide protection to Victims of acts of Violence, reporters and witnesses; and
c. carry out empowerment of Victims of acts of Violence.
2. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a include:
a. receiving complaints/reports of Violence against Women and/or Children, consultation, and counseling;
b. receiving and sending case referrals from District/City Regional Governments or institutions established by District/City Regional Governments;
c. providing legal accompaniment assistance;
d. health;
e. social rehabilitation;
f. legal services; and
g. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 15

Stages of protection for Child Victims of Violence, include: a. prevention of Violence against Children;
b. handling of Child Victims of Violence; and c. rehabilitation of Child Victims of Violence.

Article 6

Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of Violence as referred to in Article 5 shall make efforts to:
a. prevent the continuation of the criminal act;
b. provide protection to the victim;
c. provide emergency assistance; and
d. assist the complaint process and the submission of an application for a protection order.

CHAPTER VII

COMMUNITY AND BUSINESS PARTICIPATION

Article 27

1. Every person may participate in providing protection to Women and Children from acts of Violence.
2. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by:
a. preventing and stopping the occurrence of acts of Violence;
b. providing protection to Victims;
c. providing first aid to Victims of acts of Violence;
d. reporting acts of Violence against Women and/or Children;
e. socializing the rights of Women and Children independently;
f. strengthening community social institutions in handling acts of Violence against Women and Children; and/or
g. disseminating information about laws and regulations related to acts of Violence against Women and Children.

Article 7

1. The Regional Government shall carry out the Prevention of acts of Violence against Women as referred to in Article 2 paragraph (2) letter a by:
a. disseminating laws and regulations;
b. carrying out empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance, and/or counseling;
c. making efforts to increase the quantity and quality of education, both formal and non-formal and informal;
d. making efforts to build community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of Women and Children from Violence;
e. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities, religious institutions/organizations, and various non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent Violence against Women and Children;
f. establishing a Violence prevention system, and mapping locations or areas prone to Violence;
g. increasing public awareness that respects Women and protects Children;
h. establishing P2TP2A at the Regency level and Women and Children Protection Service Posts at the Sub-district, Village, and Urban Village levels; and
i. establishing a task force for the protection of Women and Children.
2. In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. the community;
c. vertical agencies;
d. law enforcement apparatus;
e. educational institutions;
f. health institutions;
g. religious institutions/organizations;
h. social community institutions; and
i. the business sector and other institutions.
3. Prevention of acts of Violence against Women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner and coordinated by the Regional Apparatus having the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 19

1. Rehabilitation of Child Victims of Violence as referred to in Article 15 letter c shall be carried out in the form of: a. legal aid services;
b. health rehabilitation services;
c. social rehabilitation services; and/or d. return and social reintegration services.
2. In the provision of rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may cooperate with: a. vertical agencies;
b. provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments; village government;
d. non-governmental organizations;
e. private parties;
f. community; and/or g. family.

Article 17

Prevention of Violence against Children in addition to being implemented by regional apparatus as referred to in Article 16 paragraph (2), also involves:

a. parents and/or family;
b. community;
c. educational institutions;
d. Child social welfare institutions; and/or e. other institutions engaged in the field of Child protection care.

1.5.text:
Article 5

The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women from acts of Violence that occur in public or private life in the form of:

a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and
h. threats of certain actions.

REGENT OF TOLITOLI,

Considering:
a. that violence against women and children is a crime against human rights so it becomes the obligation of the state to protect from all forms of violence and discrimination;
b. that to fulfill the rights of women and children as well as part of the regional government's efforts to protect women and children from exploitation, neglect and discrimination;
c. that to provide direction, foundation and legal certainty in the protection of women and children, regulation at the regional level is required;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In View of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 29 of 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions in Sulawesi (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 74, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1822);
3. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

With the Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF TOLITOLI REGENCY and

