CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 3

Women and Children who are Victims of acts of Violence have rights, including:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth respected as human beings;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable, and in accordance with needs;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain ease/facilitation in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance;
j. the right to a sense of security; and
k. the right to restitution.

Article 11

1. Health services as referred to in Article 9 paragraph (1) letter b, shall be implemented using health facilities owned by the Regional Government or other referral hospitals by providing treatment and health recovery services to Victims.
2. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be performed by health workers to Victims in accordance with professional standards, standard operating procedures and the medical needs of Victims.
3. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1), through efforts: a. taking anamnesis from the Victim;
b. examining the Victim;
c. providing first aid;
d. restoring health both physically and psychologically, undertaking efforts to prevent disease and other health disorders;
e. counseling;
f. referring to more adequate health service facilities if necessary may refer to other parties with a multidisciplinary approach, including health services, medicolegal services, psychosocial assistance and legal aid; and/or g. providing information to the police.
4. In addition to the efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) for certain cases, health workers may provide reproductive health services in accordance with medical needs.
5. In providing services as referred to in paragraph (3) and paragraph (4), health workers must create medical records in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
6. For every medical action to be performed by health workers there must be consent for the medical action from the Victim or their family in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
7. For the purposes of investigation, authorized health workers must prepare a visum et repertum and/or visum et repertum psichiatricum or prepare a medical certificate.
8. Health workers have the right to obtain legal protection.

Article 5

The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women from acts of Violence that occur in public or private life in the form of:

a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and
h. threats of certain actions.

